WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.424
Not every coding task needs
the most powerful model.

00:00:04.484 --> 00:00:09.379
A typo fix and a cross-module race
condition are very different jobs,

00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:13.019
yet most workflows send both
to the same expensive tier.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:15.372
Meet Adaptive Codex Router:

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:19.083
a local, usage-aware wrapper for the Codex CLI.

00:00:19.143 --> 00:00:23.094
It plans every task first,
then routes it to the cheapest

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:25.929
model and reasoning effort
that is likely to succeed.

00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:27.876
Step one: plan.

00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:31.416
A local Qwen3.8-27B model
analyzes your repository,

00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:36.388
so planning spends none of your Codex allowance.

00:00:36.448 --> 00:00:40.256
It returns a structured plan:
task type, relevant files,

00:00:40.316 --> 00:00:43.447
implementation steps, risks, and verification.

00:00:43.897 --> 00:00:48.049
If the local model is offline,
auto mode falls back to Luna,

00:00:48.109 --> 00:00:50.621
then to a deterministic classifier.

00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:53.605
A planner failure never blocks your coding task.

00:00:54.055 --> 00:00:55.467
Step two: route.

00:00:55.527 --> 00:00:59.995
A deterministic policy picks
the model and reasoning effort

00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:02.933
across four tiers: Luna, Terra, Sol, and Astra.

00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:05.131
A typo goes to Luna on low.

00:01:05.191 --> 00:01:07.413
A new feature gets Terra on medium.

00:01:07.473 --> 00:01:10.357
A tricky race condition goes to Sol on high.

00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:13.923
Planners can only suggest a
tier. The policy decides,

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:18.675
and Astra's most expensive settings
stay behind explicit guardrails.

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:21.518
Step three: execute and verify.

00:01:21.578 --> 00:01:25.325
The chosen model edits your
Git working tree in a sandbox,

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:28.771
and objective checks decide
whether it really succeeded.

00:01:28.831 --> 00:01:33.133
If it fails, the router looks at why
and escalates to a stronger route,

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.446
with up to three attempts by default.

00:01:35.896 --> 00:01:39.078
Every run is logged as
privacy-conscious telemetry,

00:01:39.138 --> 00:01:42.300
and prompts stay out of the
logs unless you opt in.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.359
A local dashboard shows tokens, latency, models,

00:01:45.419 --> 00:01:47.567
and attempts as they happen.

00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:50.538
And the project stays honest about evidence.

00:01:50.598 --> 00:01:54.922
On forty calibration tasks,
routing was 95% exact,

00:01:54.982 --> 00:01:56.661
with zero under-routes.

00:01:56.721 --> 00:02:00.993
A SWE-bench Verified harness is
ready, but no savings are claimed

00:02:01.053 --> 00:02:04.253
until at least one hundred
real tasks are measured.

00:02:04.703 --> 00:02:06.499
Adaptive Codex Router.

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.384
Plan locally, route smartly,

00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:10.976
and pay only for the reasoning you need.
